    "q-52203": {
      "dom_id": "step-q52203",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{2}{7}y=2 \\\\ -x+\\frac{2}{9}y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $7$ and the second equation by $9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{2}{7}y=2 & |\\times 7 \\\\ -x+\\frac{2}{9}y=2 & |\\times 9\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}7x-2y=14 \\\\ -9x+2y=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $y$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-\\cancel{2y} & =14 \\\\ -9x+\\cancel{2y} & =18 \\\\ (7x-9x) & =(14+18) \\\\ -2x & =32 \\\\ x & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-16$ into the first equation to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-2y & =14 \\\\ 7(-16)-2y & =14 \\\\ -112-2y & =14 \\\\ -2y & =126 \\\\ y & =-63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-16$ and $y=-63.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
