    "q-75861": {
      "dom_id": "step-q75861",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{2}y=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $6,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=-1 & |\\times 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{2}y=-6 & |\\times 6\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=-4 \\\\ 2x+3y=-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=-4 & |\\,\\times (-1) \\\\ 2x+3y=-36 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=4 \\\\ 2x+3y=-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable, and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{-2x}+y & =4 \\\\ \\cancel{2x}+3y & =-36 \\\\ (y+3y) & =(4-36) \\\\ 4y & =-32 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{32}{4} \\\\ y & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=-8$ into the first equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =-4 \\\\ 2x-(-8) & =-4 \\\\ 2x+8 & =-4 \\\\ 2x & =-12 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{12}{2} \\\\ x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-6,\\,y=-8.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
