    "q-216312": {
      "dom_id": "step-q216312",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}p+3q=25 \\\\ p+\\frac{2}{5}q=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}p+3q=25 & |\\times 4 \\\\ p+\\frac{2}{5}q=0 & |\\times 5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5p+12q=100 \\\\ 5p+2q=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $-5p$ term that can cancel with the $5p$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5p+12q=100 & |\\,\\times (-1) \\\\ 5p+2q=0 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-5p-12q=-100 \\\\ 5p+2q=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $p$-variable, and allows us to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{-5p}-12q & =-100 \\\\ \\cancel{5p}+2q & =0 \\\\ (-12q+2q) & =(-100+0) \\\\ -10q & =-100 \\\\ q & =\\frac{100}{10} \\\\ q & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $q=10$ into the second equation to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5p+2q & =0 \\\\ 5p+2\\cdot 10 & =0 \\\\ 5p+20 & =0 \\\\ 5p & =-20 \\\\ p & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $p=-4,\\,q=10.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
